    <header class="case-hero">
  <h1>Telia’s Help & Guides<br>UX Analysis & Prototypes</h1>
  <p class="meta">UX Research • UX/UI • 2024 · Women in Tech program</p>
  <p class="lede">
    Evaluated the Help & Guides section of Telia’s mobile app, identified two core
    usability problems, and presented research-backed solutions and prototypes to the
    Telia team. One recommended pattern later appeared in production.
  </p>
</header>

<!-- Hero preview -->
<section class="hero-vertical" aria-label="Featured preview">
  <button class="shot-tile ar-21x9"
    data-full="/images/telia/telia-hero.webp"
    data-title="Help & Guides - prototype overview"
    data-cap="<p>Prioritized two key problems and delivered targeted solutions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simplified information architecture and entry points.</li>
      <li>Guided, step-by-step flow for common help tasks.</li>
      <li>Clearer copy and visual hierarchy for faster scanning.</li>
      <li>Usability-tested interactive prototypes in Figma.</li>
    </ul>">
    <img src="/images/telia/telia-hero.webp" alt="Proposed Help & Guides flow for Telia’s mobile app">
  </button>
</section>

<section id="s-architecture">
  <h2>Context & Problem</h2>
  <p>
    As part of the <em>Women in Tech</em> learning program, our team partnered with Telia to review the
    Help & Guides section of their mobile app. Customer support tasks (e.g., troubleshooting, plan details,
    SIM & device topics) were spread across multiple entry points with inconsistent wording and
    hierarchy. Users struggled to decide where to start and how to complete common tasks without
    bouncing between screens.
  </p>
  <p>We framed two primary problems to solve:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fragmented navigation & IA:</strong> parallel paths and duplicate entry points created decision friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Factory-reset instructions:</strong> the section was nearly unusable on mobile due to weak hierarchy and unclear steps.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="s-personas">
  <h2>Personas & Testing Insights</h2>
  <p>
    We based our testing scenarios on two primary personas representing Telia’s customer base:
    <strong>Kersti</strong> - a self-reliant rural user who values simplicity and quick mobile solutions.
    And <strong>Andrus</strong> - an experienced urban professional who expects reliable, clear guidance when resolving issues.
  </p>
  <p>
    Guided by their needs, we designed realistic tasks such as locating troubleshooting steps and performing
    a factory reset. Sessions were kept neutral to avoid leading participants and to preserve natural discovery.
    This revealed friction in labeling and navigation and confirmed that
    <strong>a simple step-by-step flow</strong> provided the highest success and confidence across both personas.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="s-approach">
  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ran <strong>user interviews</strong> and <strong>scenario-based usability tests</strong> on common help tasks, avoiding leading questions or over-guidance.</li>
    <li>Performed a <strong>heuristic evaluation</strong> and content audit to surface usability and accessibility gaps.</li>
    <li>Mapped a simplified <strong>information architecture</strong> with a clear single entry point for Help & Guides.</li>
    <li>Designed a <strong>guided, step-by-step mobile flow</strong> for troubleshooting and factory reset using progressive disclosure.</li>
    <li>Rewrote microcopy for <strong>clarity and recovery</strong>, making each step actionable.</li>
    <li>Built and tested interactive <strong>Figma prototypes</strong>, iterating on participant feedback.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <strong>simplest solution - a clearly structured step-by-step help flow</strong> aligned with users’ mental models.
    I designed this approach, and a version of it was ultimately <strong>chosen and implemented by Telia</strong> after our presentation.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="s-outcomes">
  <h2>Outcomes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduced choice overload with a consolidated entry and clearer IA.</li>
    <li>Introduced a guided flow that <strong>shortened common tasks</strong> and reduced back-and-forth navigation.</li>
    <li>Improved learnability with consistent labels, headings, and empty/error states.</li>
    <li>The step-by-step mobile pattern was <strong>later implemented in the live app</strong>, validating the research insights.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="s-role">
  <h2>My Role</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Led the <strong>heuristic review</strong> and co-led <strong>user research</strong> and synthesis.</li>
    <li>Produced <strong>wireflows and interactive prototypes</strong> for the two priority problems.</li>
    <li>Prepared and delivered the <strong>stakeholder presentation</strong> to Telia’s design team.</li>
    <li>Documented IA changes, copy recommendations, and next steps for validation.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<p class="flex-col links-container">
